"Below the crust is the mantle, the thickest layer by far. It reaches down to 2,900 kilometers and makes up more than four fifths of the Earth. The mantle is solid rock, and it is very hot: 2,000 to 3,000 degrees Celsius. Over millions of years it flows, very slowly, and this slow movement helps push the plates of the crust around.": "3448696bb671",
"But if no one has ever been deeper than 12 kilometers, how do we know any of this? The answer is earthquakes. Earthquake waves travel all the way through the Earth, and they change speed and direction when the rock changes. One kind, the S wave, cannot pass through liquid at all. By recording the waves all over the world, scientists can work out what lies below.": "d3f219f445a3",
"Check the air pressure in the tires.": "e169f09cb0f6",
"How do scientists know what is inside the Earth?": "3c1f105f2096",
"How far down can people dig? The deepest hole ever drilled is in the far north of Russia. Scientists worked on it for many years, and in 1989 it reached 12,262 meters, a little more than 12 kilometers. That sounds deep. But the center of the Earth is 6,371 kilometers below your feet. The hole did not even get through the outer skin.": "f229077ea720",
"How far down can people dig? The deepest hole ever drilled is in the far north of Russia. Scientists worked on it for many years, and in 1989 it reached 12,262 meters, a little more than 12 kilometers. That sounds deep. But the center of the Earth is 6,371 kilometers below your feet. The hole did not even get through the outer skin. That outer skin is called the crust. It is the hard rock we live on, and it lies under the oceans too. The crust is from 5 to 70 kilometers thick, and about 17 kilometers on average. If the Earth were an apple, the crust would be thinner than the peel. Below the crust is the mantle, the thickest layer by far. It reaches down to 2,900 kilometers and makes up more than four fifths of the Earth. The mantle is solid rock, and it is very hot: 2,000 to 3,000 degrees Celsius. Over millions of years it flows, very slowly, and this slow movement helps push the plates of the crust around. Under the mantle is the core, and it has two parts. The outer core is a deep ocean of liquid iron and nickel. As this metal moves, it makes the Earth's magnetic field. At the very center is the inner core, a ball of iron about 1,251 kilometers in radius. It is even hotter than the outer core, yet it is solid, because the pressure there is so great. But if no one has ever been deeper than 12 kilometers, how do we know any of this? The answer is earthquakes. Earthquake waves travel all the way through the Earth, and they change speed and direction when the rock changes. One kind, the S wave, cannot pass through liquid at all. By recording the waves all over the world, scientists can work out what lies below. In 1909 a scientist named Andrija Mohorovičić found the bottom of the crust this way. In 1936 Inge Lehmann studied earthquake records and discovered that the core has a solid center. Every large earthquake still teaches us something. We have never seen the inside of our planet, but we have learned to listen to it.": "0c20b722ba4e",
"How is the outer core different from the inner core?": "48b09232b15e",
